\section{Recognizing surveys, the module \texttt{surveys}}\label{surveys}

Assignment groups often hold mandatory surveys next to the graded work: a
course evaluation at the end of a seminar series, say.
A survey's score says nothing about the student.
Canvas awards a classic graded survey its full points on submission, whatever
the answers, so every student who answered a 100-point course evaluation has
the grade [[100]]---and a teacher who lowers the survey to 50 points silently
changes every one of those grades to [[50]].
A summary module that reads a survey's grade therefore decides a student's
result by an accident of how Canvas scores surveys.

The only thing a survey can tell us is whether the student completed it.
Every summary module in this chapter treats a survey the same way:
\begin{itemize}
\item a completed survey counts as passed (P), an uncompleted one as failed;
\item a survey never contributes a letter to an A--E average or maximum;
\item [[missing_assignments]] reports an uncompleted survey and never a
  completed one, so that [[--missing]] agrees with the summary.
\end{itemize}
This module holds what the summary modules need for that and nothing else:
the predicate [[is_survey]] that tells a survey from graded work, the
predicate [[survey_completed]] that tells whether a student completed it, and
the [[--missing]] report for surveys.
