\subsection{What makes an assignment a survey}

An earlier version of this predicate, then in [[maxgradesurvey]], classified
by the assignment's grading scale: an assignment that is not graded with
letters ([[grading_type]] other than [[letter_grade]] or [[gpa_scale]]) was
a survey.
That confuses the grading scale with the kind of assignment.
Points are how Canvas grades a survey, but they are also how it grades
plenty of real work: a FeedbackFruits peer review graded out of 100 points
was a \enquote{survey} under that rule, and a student who merely submitted it
passed without being assessed.
Conversely, nothing stops a teacher from grading a survey with letters, and
then the rule missed it.

Whether an assignment is a survey is a property of the quiz behind it.
Canvas's classic quizzes carry a [[quiz_type]]: [[assignment]] for a graded
quiz, [[practice_quiz]], [[graded_survey]] and [[survey]].
Graded quizzes and graded surveys create assignments; practice quizzes and
ungraded surveys normally don't, but if an ungraded survey ever reaches a
summary module it is no less a survey, so we accept both survey types.
<<constants>>=
SURVEY_QUIZ_TYPES = {"graded_survey", "survey"}
@

An assignment points to its classic quiz through [[quiz_id]], and its
[[submission_types]] is [[["online_quiz"]]].
Canvas sends both together, but we accept either: an assignment with
[[online_quiz]] and no [[quiz_id]] (an object built by a teacher's own
script, say) can still be matched to its quiz by the quiz's
[[assignment_id]].
<<functions>>=
def is_classic_quiz_assignment(assignment):
  """True iff the assignment is backed by a classic Canvas quiz."""
  return (getattr(assignment, "quiz_id", None) is not None
          or getattr(assignment, "submission_types", None) == ["online_quiz"])
@

The [[getattr]] calls are deliberate.
The CLI normalizes the assignment attributes it uses at its ingress helpers
(the attribute lists in [[canvaslms.cli.utils]]), but [[quiz_id]] is not
among them, and the summary modules also receive assignments from teachers'
own scripts and from older cached objects.
An assignment that lacks the attributes cannot be backed by a quiz we could
find, so it is not a survey.

\paragraph{New Quizzes}
Canvas's New Quizzes gained surveys, graded and ungraded, in December 2025.
We cannot recognize them.
The New Quizzes API documents no quiz type at all: its quiz object has an id,
a title, a grading type, points, dates and a [[quiz_settings]] object of
delivery options (calculator, shuffling, time limit, access code and the
like).
A read-only look at the sandbox course confirmed that the API returns exactly
those fields, and that the assignment behind a New Quiz says only that it is
one ([[is_quiz_lti_assignment]], [[submission_types]] of
[[["external_tool"]]]), not what kind.
So a New Quiz is never a survey here, and a graded New Quizzes survey is
summarized like any other graded assignment.
A course that wants the completion rule for a survey should use a classic
graded survey.

\paragraph{Looking up the quiz}
With a classic quiz assignment in hand, we fetch its quiz to read the type.
The summary modules call [[is_survey]] once per student and assignment---both
[[summarize]] and [[missing_assignments]] loop over the students---so a group
of $N$ students would fetch the same quiz $N$ times.
We therefore remember each quiz's type.
The summary modules get nothing but assignment and user lists, so there is
no per-run object to keep this memory in; a module-level dictionary keyed by
the quiz id serves, since a quiz id identifies one quiz on a Canvas instance
and the process lives only as long as one CLI run.
(The persisted Canvas cache also caches [[get_quiz]], but it
is off under [[--no-cache]] and in teachers' own scripts, and even when on it
answers per call, not per quiz.)
An assignment that has no [[quiz_id]] is remembered by its own id instead.
<<constants>>=
quiz_type_cache = {}
<<functions>>=
def is_survey(assignment):
  """True iff the assignment is a classic Canvas quiz of survey type.

  The quiz is fetched through the assignment's course once per quiz; later
  calls for the same quiz are answered from `quiz_type_cache`. New Quizzes
  are never surveys: Canvas exposes no survey marker for them.
  """
  if not is_classic_quiz_assignment(assignment):
    return False

  quiz_id = getattr(assignment, "quiz_id", None)
  if quiz_id is not None:
    key = ("quiz", quiz_id)
  else:
    key = ("assignment", assignment.id)

  if key not in quiz_type_cache:
    quiz_type_cache[key] = fetch_quiz_type(assignment, quiz_id)

  return quiz_type_cache[key] in SURVEY_QUIZ_TYPES
@

The quiz lives in the assignment's course.
The [[results]] command attaches that course to every assignment as
[[assignment.course]]; an assignment from a teacher's own script may not have
it, and then we build the course from [[course_id]] with the assignment's own
connection to Canvas.

A quiz that no longer exists ([[ResourceDoesNotExist]]) gives no type, so the
assignment is not a survey.
That is the safe direction of the two possible mistakes.
Taking graded work for a survey passes a student on mere submission---the
very bug that motivated this module.
Taking a survey for graded work does no such harm: its score of [[100]] is
either a passing grade (in [[participation]]) or an unknown grade that the
module reports as an error, so the teacher hears about it.
Any other error, such as a lost connection, propagates: the summary cannot
be trusted without the answer.
<<functions>>=
def fetch_quiz_type(assignment, quiz_id=None):
  """Return the `quiz_type` of the classic quiz behind the assignment.

  Returns None if the quiz cannot be found.
  """
  course = getattr(assignment, "course", None)
  if course is None:
    course = canvasapi.course.Course(assignment._requester,
                                     {"id": assignment.course_id})

  try:
    if quiz_id is not None:
      quiz = course.get_quiz(quiz_id)
    else:
      quiz = next((quiz for quiz in course.get_quizzes()
                   if getattr(quiz, "assignment_id", None) == assignment.id),
                  None)
  except ResourceDoesNotExist:
    return None

  return getattr(quiz, "quiz_type", None)
@

